  "query_preview": "..\nToday: In the wake of the cold frontal passage yesterday, \ntemperatures today will be 8-10 degrees cooler than normal for this \ntime of year. Highs will be in the mid to high 70s. Wind gusts \nremain breezy throughout the basin (20 to 25 mph) and gusty in the \nCascade gaps (30 to 35 mph). Though these winds are weaker than \nyesterday, when paired with relative humidity values of 20 to 30 \npercent, result in enhanced fire weather concerns today. Main \nimpacts will be the potential for new and ongoing fires to spread \nrapidly due to these winds. Additionally, in the Cascade gaps, \nblowing objects and driving may be difficult, especially for high \nprofile vehicles. This evening, a trough will swing down through the \narea, shifting winds to mostly from the north. These northerly winds \nwill funnel down the Okanogan Valley and into the deeper basin and \nWaterville Plateau areas, which will see wind gusts last into Monday \nmorning. Winds in the Spokane/Coeur d'Alene area and southward will \ndecrease after midnight. The trough will result in some instability, \nbut little if any rain will reach the ground due to the dry air mass \nushered in by the cold front. Still, the potential for sprinkles has \nbeen introduced to the forecast for tonight.  \n\nMonday through Friday: Once the trough moves out of the area, dry \nnorthwest flow will move in as a ridge builds along the PNW coast. \nNearly 100 percent of clusters support ridging through at least \nmidweek. This will result in dry conditions with mostly weak winds, \nand will lead to wildfire smoke and haze issues during the week. \nAdditionally, temperatures will warm back into the 90s by Wednesday \nand much of the region seeing a return of Moderate HeatRisk on \nThursday. Winds along the Cascade gaps will be seasonably breezy, \nwith gusts 15-20 mph expected each afternoon and evening. Near the \nend of the workweek and into next weekend, the ridge will flatten \nand a surface low dropping into Montana will strengthen the pressure \ngradient across the Cascades. This will bring stronger winds to the \nCascade gaps on Thursday, and more widespread winds to the basin and \nforecast area Friday. With wind gusts 20-25 mph anticipated and \nlocalized wind gusts 30-35 mph, Friday is the next day being eyed \nfor elevated fire weather concerns. There is a near zero chance for \nany precipitation within the next week for all areas except the \nCascade gaps, so expect little to no relief from dry conditions. /AS",
